els/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n42\t- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n43\t- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n44\t- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n45\t\n46\t### MCP / RPC\n47\t\n48\t- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n49\t- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n50\t- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n51\t\n52\t### Extension (TypeScript)\n53\t\n54\t- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n55\t- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n56\t- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n57\t\n58\t### Tests\n59\t\n60\t- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n61\t- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n62\t- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n63\t\n64\t### Architecture docs already present\n65\t\n66\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n67\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n68\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n69\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n70\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n71\t\n72\tReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n77\t\n78\t| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n79\t|---|---|---|\n80\t| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n81\t| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n82\t| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n83\t| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n84\t| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n85\t| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n86\t| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n87\t| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n88\t| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n89\t| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n90\t| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n91\t| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n96\t\n97\tThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n98\t\n99\tCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n100\t\n101\t- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n102\t- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n103\t- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n104\t\n105\t**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first taspace-fork-build/tasks.json","offset":998,"limit":285},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":450,"cache_read_input_tokens":47238,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":450,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":47,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6e1e0b3d-59f0-45b5-814f-93ff7d5e5a5b","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mkg7mEy6vQ2d3xbFwx","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2038:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2691:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:547:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WhKM9reGdKdCKZb74cYSmh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n2\t\n3\t**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n4\t**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n5\t**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n6\t\n7\tThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. What the spec actually demands\n12\t\n13\tThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n14\t\n15\tThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n16\t\n17\t**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n18\t\n19\t- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n20\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n21\t- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n22\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n23\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n24\t- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n25\t- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n26\t- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n27\t- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n28\t- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n33\t\n34\t### Daemon (Rust)\n35\t\n36\t- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n37\t- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n38\t- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n39\t- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n40\t- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n41\t- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n42\t- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n43\t- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n44\t- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n45\t\n46\t### MCP / RPC\n47\t\n48\t- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n49\t- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n50\t- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n51\t\n52\t### Extension (TypeScript)\n53\t\n54\t- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n55\t- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n56\t- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n57\t\n58\t### Tests\n59\t\n60\t- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n61\t- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n62\t- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n63\t\n64\t### Architecture docs already present\n65\t\n66\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n67\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n68\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n69\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n70\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n71\t\n72\tReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n77\t\n78\t| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n79\t|---|---|---|\n80\t| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n81\t| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n82\t| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n83\t| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n84\t| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n85\t| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n86\t| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n87\t| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n88\t| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n89\t| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n90\t| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n91\t| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n96\t\n97\tThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n98\t\n99\tCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n100\t\n101\t- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n102\t- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n103\t- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n104\t\n105\t**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n110\t\n111\t| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n112\t|---|---|---|\n113\t| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n114\t| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n115\t| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n116\t| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n117\t| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n118\t\n119\t---\n120\t\n121\t## 6. Verification discipline\n122\t\n123\tEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n124\t\n125\t- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n126\t- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n127\t- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n128\t- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n129\t- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n130\t- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n131\t- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n132\t\n133\tIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 7. Sequencing rationale\n138\t\n139\tThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n140\t\n141\t1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n142\t2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n143\t3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n144\t4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n145\t5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n146\t6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n147\t7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n148\t8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n149\t\n150\tNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n151\t\n152\t---\n153\t\n154\t## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n155\t\n156\tThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n157\t\n158\tThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2286115f-f5be-472c-a8a6-d87768c1eefa","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:33:24.491Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2058:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2714:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:639:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01M8uBUCTUJMj3USgaU3iXWd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CF386hStJ13LMHcfm7LVJ2","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T39.md","content":"# T39 — Phase 6 — Consolidation job runtime + proposal model (no silent writes)\n\n**Phase:** 6\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R38\n**Spec anchor:** [§Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#6-consolidation-engine), [§Phase 6: Consolidation Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-6-consolidation-engine), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task stands up the consolidation engine's runtime kernel and the proposal model on which every later Phase 6 job depends. It introduces the `consolidation_jobs` table from spec §Storage Design, the bounded job queue, and a strict invariant that no consolidation job — deterministic or LLM-driven — writes durable memory directly. Every job emits a `ConsolidationProposal` record that requires explicit `apply` or `reject` before any memory state changes. Without this kernel, the LLM-driven jobs in T42 would be free to silently mutate memory, violating spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties and the §Opaque Memory Mutation risk control.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"No consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\"\n\n> \"each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\"\n\n> \"consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\"\n\n> \"Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\" (§Non-Negotiable Product Properties)\n\n> Core tables include `consolidation_jobs` (§Storage Design).\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R38 Phase 5 working-memory review — the consolidation runtime hangs off the same `.lattice/` storage layout and reuses the event-log and memory-graph identity types finalized through Phases 1–5.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — current memory store API; proposals must wrap writes through the existing store rather than bypass it.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — SQLite connection setup, WAL mode, migration conventions.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — `EventEnvelope` (`MemoryConsolidated`, consolidation failure events) emitted by job lifecycle hooks.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/` — `MemoryId`, `EventId`, `WorkspaceId` used inside proposal payloads.\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md` §1 (proposal-only invariant, bounded queue) and §6 (verification discipline).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Error handling, §Schema / model parity, §No broken windows; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references (cite §Consolidation Engine and §Storage Design rather than re-explaining behavior in code comments).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Consolidation Engine, §Phase 6, and §Storage Design in full; record the proposal-only invariant verbatim in the top-of-module doc comment on `consolidation/mod.rs`.\n2. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/mod.rs` re-exporting `proposal`, `queue`, and the `ConsolidationJobRuntime` entry point. The module doc comment must cite the exact `## 6. Consolidation Engine` and `## Storage Design` headings.\n3. Author `consolidation/schema.sql` defining the `consolidation_jobs` table (columns: `job_id PK`, `workspace_id`, `kind`, `mode` ∈ {`synchronous_post_task`, `background`, `manual_review`, `replay`}, `status` ∈ {`queued`, `running`, `proposed`, `applied`, `rejected`, `failed`, `dropped`}, `enqueued_at`, `started_at`, `finished_at`, `proposal_id NULLABLE`, `error_kind NULLABLE`, indexes on `(workspace_id, status)` and `(kind, status)`) plus a `consolidation_proposals` table (columns: `proposal_id PK`, `job_id FK`, `target_memory_id NULLABLE`, `proposal_kind`, `prior_state JSON`, `proposed_state JSON`, `evidence JSON`, `decided_at NULLABLE`, `decision` ∈ {`pending`, `applied`, `rejected`}, `decided_by NULLABLE`).\n4. In `consolidation/proposal.rs`, define `pub struct ConsolidationProposal { proposal_id, job_id, target: ProposalTarget, prior_state, proposed_state, evidence }`, the `ProposalKind` enum (e.g. `CreateMemory`, `UpdateMemory`, `MarkStale`, `Supersede`, `Demote`, `Refresh`), and the `ProposalDecision` enum. Implement `apply()` and `reject()` methods that mutate `consolidation_proposals.decision`, emit a `MemoryConsolidated` event with prior + proposed state preserved, and only then call the memory store API to materialize the change. `apply` MUST be a no-op if `decision != pending`.\n5. In `consolidation/queue.rs`, implement `BoundedJobQueue` with a configurable `max_depth` (default from `LatticeConfig`, no hard-coded literals). When `enqueue` is called and the queue is full, mark the job as `dropped` in `consolidation_jobs`, emit a `tracing::warn!` log including `workspace_id`, `kind`, and current depth, and return an `EnqueueOutcome::Dropped { reason }` — never block. Expose `depth()`, `pop()`, and `drain_for_shutdown()` for observability.\n6. Implement `ConsolidationJobRuntime` with `submit(job_spec)`, `run_due()` (polled by the daemon's scheduler), `decide(proposal_id, decision)` and `replay_from(event_id)`. The runtime never writes memory; it only writes proposals and dispatches their decisions.\n7. Wire `mod consolidation;` into `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/lib.rs` (alphabetical order) and register `consolidation/schema.sql` in the storage migration runner so the tables are created on daemon start.\n8. In `consolidation/proposal_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) a deterministic job emits a proposal but does not mutate memory, (b) `apply` materializes the change and writes a `MemoryConsolidated` event with prior + proposed state, (c) `reject` leaves memory unchanged and records the decision, (d) double-apply is a no-op, (e) the queue drops jobs once depth exceeds the bound and emits the warning log (capture via a tracing subscriber fixture), (f) the `consolidation_jobs` schema round-trips under the migration runner against a temp DB.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/mod.rs` — module root; re-exports `ConsolidationJobRuntime`, `ConsolidationProposal`, `ProposalKind`, `ProposalDecision`, `BoundedJobQueue`, `EnqueueOutcome`; doc comment cites spec §Consolidation Engine and §Storage Design.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/proposal.rs` — `ConsolidationProposal` struct, `ProposalKind`/`ProposalDecision` enums, `apply`/`reject` methods, prior-state preservation, event emission.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/queue.rs` — `BoundedJobQueue` with configurable depth, drop-with-warning behavior, observability hooks.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/schema.sql` — `consolidation_jobs` and `consolidation_proposals` tables with the exact columns and indexes the runtime requires.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/proposal_tests.rs` — unit tests for proposal lifecycle, no-silent-writes, bounded-queue drops, schema migration round-trip.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/mod.rs` — confirms the module root exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/proposal.rs` — confirms the proposal module exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib consolidation::proposal_tests` — every proposal-runtime unit test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. Split `proposal.rs` or `queue.rs` if any approaches the ceiling (e.g. extract a `decision.rs` sub-module).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. `BoundedJobQueue::enqueue` and `ConsolidationProposal::apply` are the hot spots — keep them flat with early returns.\n- Single source of truth: proposals MUST route writes through the existing memory store API (`crates/lattice-core/src/memory/`) — do not introduce a parallel write path.\n- Error handling: queue-full and apply-on-non-pending are normal control flow (typed `EnqueueOutcome` / `ApplyOutcome`), not panics. No silent `.ok()` drops on storage results.\n- Naming: `ConsolidationProposal`, `ProposalDecision`, `BoundedJobQueue` — intent over mechanism; no generic `Manager`/`Helper` suffixes.\n- Schema parity: every column on `consolidation_jobs` and `consolidation_proposals` has a typed field on the Rust struct; nullable columns map to `Option<…>`.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Observability: every proposal lifecycle transition emits a `tracing` span carrying `workspace_id`, `job_id`, `proposal_id`, and outcome. Queue drops log at `warn` per spec.\n- Doc citation: top-of-module doc comments cite `## 6. Consolidation Engine` and `## Storage Design` headings; per-method comments reference these headings only where behavior is non-obvious (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: deterministic jobs (T41), LLM jobs (T42), provenance (T43), manual review (T44), replay (T45), and integration tests (T46) are sibling tasks — but the proposal-only invariant, bounded queue, and `consolidation_jobs` schema all ship in this task.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3709,"cache_read_input_tokens":53202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3709,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015qAoQeXM7HFtFDzBbPkUNq","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"9c71f470-98b9-4480-8f0d-0c8ccdebc756","request_id":"req_011Cb7MmqeXLr2UsR7g5bqCY","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 6+7+Gate task briefs"}
