docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T85.md:41:5. Create `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-consolidation-design.md`. The authoritative reference for the consolidation engine. Required headings: `## Job types`, `## Modes` (synchronous / background / manual-review / replay), `## LLM-driven consolidation` (proposal-only, provenance, queue depth, cost budgets per spec §6), `## Proposal apply and reject flow`, `## Reversibility`, `## Replay-safe execution`.
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R05.log:11:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013SN72EXjZoKVV7PgLUBYLw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4cb46183-2e7c-4dbc-b888-4ae03f7bba08","uuid":"d5e243ea-3d28-4697-91ea-bce60d5527b4","timestamp":"2026-05-17T03:27:41.328Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-05-16\n**Status:** Proposed\n**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n\nThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n\nThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n\n- code graph\n- document graph\n- event log\n- typed memory graph\n- workflow engine\n- consolidation engine\n- verification engine\n- explainable retrieval and ranking\n- assistant usefulness metrics\n\nThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n\n## Design Thesis\n\nThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n\n1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n\nThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n\n## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n\n### A-Mem\n\nA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n\n- atomic memory notes\n- generated context, tags, and keywords\n- embeddings for semantic access\n- explicit memory-to-memory links\n- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n\nAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n\n### AgeMem\n\nAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n\n- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n\nDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n\n### ViLoMem\n\nViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n\n- code topology\n- workflow episodes\n- failure patterns\n- semantic repo claims\n- architecture decisions\n- user/team preferences\n- docs and contract state\n\nEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n\n### SRMA-Style Reflection\n\nUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n\n- reflection quality\n- consolidation quality\n- retention quality\n- drift detection\n- retrieval alignment\n\nDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n\n- Local-first by default.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence.\n- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Ingestion Layer\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- watch workspace changes\n- parse code\n- parse docs\n- parse config and build files\n- ingest test results and diagnostics\n- capture assistant event traces\n- normalize all inputs into stable records\n\nRequired inputs:\n\n- source files\n- Markdown docs\n- repo instruction files\n- test files and test manifests\n- package/build metadata\n- compiler diagnostics\n- test runner output\n- MCP tool calls and responses\n- assistant edits and generated diffs\n- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n\nThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n\n### 2. Workspace Graph\n\nThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n\nPrimary node families:\n\n- `File`\n- `Directory`\n- `Symbol`\n- `Type`\n- `Module`\n- `Test`\n- `Document`\n- `Section`\n- `ConfigKey`\n- `Command`\n- `Route`\n- `Schema`\n- `Package`\n- `BuildTarget`\n- `RuntimeSurface`\n\nPrimary edge families:\n\n- `contains`\n- `imports`\n- `calls`\n- `implements`\n- `extends`\n- `type_ref`\n- `tested_by`\n- `documents`\n- `mentions`\n- `depends_on`\n- `configured_by`\n- `generated_by`\n- `co_changed_with`\n- `stale_against`\n\nThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n\n### 3. Event Log\n\nThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n\nRequired event types:\n\n- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n- `ToolCalled`\n- `ToolResult`\n- `ContextBundleReturned`\n- `MemoryRetrieved`\n- `MemoryExpanded`\n- `PlanCreated`\n- `FileRead`\n- `PatchApplied`\n- `TestRunStarted`\n- `TestRunCompleted`\n- `DiagnosticObserved`\n- `UserCorrection`\n- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n- `WorkflowFailed`\n- `MemoryCreated`\n- `MemoryUpdated`\n- `MemoryInvalidated`\n- `MemoryConsolidated`\n\nEvents should carry:\n\n- workspace id\n- branch\n- session id\n- task id\n- actor\n- timestamp\n- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n- payload hash\n- compact payload summary\n- optional full payload location\n\nThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n\n### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n\nConsolidation modes:\n\n- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n\nNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n\nLLM-driven consolidation:\n\nSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n\n- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n\n### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n\nVerification checks:\n\n- linked files still exist\n- linked symbols still exist\n- cited docs still exist\n- linked tests still exist\n- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n- time-bound memory has expired\n\nVerification outputs:\n\n- `verified`\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n\n### 9. MCP Surface\n\nThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n\nRequired workflow tools:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`\n- `prepare_change`\n- `plan_edit`\n- `trace_scenario`\n- `diagnose_failure`\n- `find_relevant_tests`\n- `impact_from_diff`\n- `get_docs_capsule`\n- `get_backlinks`\n- `expand_context`\n- `get_working_set_context`\n- `get_repo_playbook`\n- `search_symbols`\n- `search_memory`\n- `record_workflow_outcome`\n\nNew or redesigned memory tools:\n\n- `get_task_memory`\n- `save_memory`\n- `propose_memory_evolution`\n- `apply_memory_evolution`\n- `verify_memory`\n- `explain_memory`\n- `list_memory_conflicts`\n- `consolidate_session`\n- `get_memory_metrics`\n- `get_event_trace`\n\nTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n\nEvery tool response should support:\n\n- compact rendering\n- full structured JSON\n- context handles\n- stable expansion targets\n- budget controls\n- diagnostic explanations where useful\n\n### 10. Human Review Surface\n\nAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n\nRequired operator views:\n\n- memory inbox\n- proposed promotions\n- proposed contradictions\n- stale memory list\n- memory evidence view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- usefulness metrics\n- workspace graph health\n- indexing health\n- consolidation queue\n\nThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n\n## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n\n### Phase 11: Hardening\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- large-repo performance tests\n- concurrency tests\n- recovery tests\n- corrupted-event handling\n- partial-index handling\n- workspace-boundary tests\n- migration tests\n- MCP schema compatibility tests\n- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n\n## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n\nEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n\n- `overview`\n- ranked pivots\n- relevant context\n- memory highlights\n- event episodes where relevant\n- suggested next expansion\n- stable handles\n- risks or uncertainty\n- compact/full render choice\n- structured payload\n\nEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n\n- why the memory was included\n- evidence strength\n- verification status\n- freshness status\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- scope\n- expansion target\n\nEvery workflow tool should record:\n\n- inputs\n- resolved anchors\n- selected candidates\n- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n- response summary\n- downstream use events\n\n## Documentation Requirements\n\nRequired docs:\n\n- successor architecture overview\n- MCP contract reference\n- memory model reference\n- event log design\n- consolidation design\n- retrieval/ranking design\n- verification/freshness design\n- operator guide\n- migration guide from Lattice\n- benchmark/evaluation guide\n- extension review UI guide\n\nDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\nRequired test families:\n\n- storage migration tests\n- event append/replay tests\n- graph identity tests\n- parser tests\n- memory link tests\n- contradiction/supersession tests\n- stale verification tests\n- retrieval ranking golden tests\n- workflow golden tests\n- MCP schema tests\n- compact/full render tests\n- concurrency tests\n- large fixture performance tests\n- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n\nNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n\n## Measurable Success Criteria\n\nThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n\n- lower tool calls per coding task\n- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n- higher memory reuse precision\n- lower stale memory surfacing\n- better repeated-failure avoidance\n- better test recommendation accuracy\n- better user correction retention\n- faster diagnosis from failures\n- more explainable context selection\n\nInitial targets:\n\n- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n\n## Risks\n\n### Opaque Memory Mutation\n\nRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n\nControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n\n### Ranking Complexity\n\nRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n\nControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n\n### Event Log Growth\n\nRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n\nControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n\n### Stale Memory Leakage\n\nRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n\nControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n\n### Scope Leakage\n\nRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n\nControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n\n### Overfitting To Current Repo\n\nRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n\nControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n\nImplement in this order:\n\n1. Event model and append-only store.\n2. MCP workflow event capture.\n3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n4. Memory access/use history.\n5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n\nThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n\n## Final Target\n\nThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n\n- what the repo contains\n- why the repo is shaped that way\n- what was tried before\n- what failed before\n- what tests prove behavior\n- what memories are trustworthy\n- what memories are stale\n- what context matters now\n- what should be done next\n\nThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n","numLines":969,"startLine":1,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2613:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:293:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01UVmYnr3gHaH6rTbzMc6EN9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0147XV4VLMFkb6ECM3CEQHZg","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md","content":"# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n\n**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n\nThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the spec actually demands\n\nThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n\nThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n\n**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n\n- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n\n### Daemon (Rust)\n\n- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n\n### MCP / RPC\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n\n### Extension (TypeScript)\n\n- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n\n### Architecture docs already present\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n\nReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n\n| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n\nThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n\nCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n\n- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n\n**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n\n| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification discipline\n\nEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n\n- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n\nIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sequencing rationale\n\nThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n\n1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n\nNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n\nThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n\nThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":681,"cache_read_input_tokens":85471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":681},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2f8ef79d-b4b6-49da-8599-4f462a836578","request_id":"req_011Cb7LwMJ7arNp4ALKuNFMw"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2614:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:294:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0147XV4VLMFkb6ECM3CEQHZg","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"b1ef366b-0493-47fa-af95-c66339ba4c7f","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:24:01.804Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md","content":"# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n\n**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n\nThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the spec actually demands\n\nThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n\nThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n\n**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n\n- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n\n### Daemon (Rust)\n\n- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n\n### MCP / RPC\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n\n### Extension (TypeScript)\n\n- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n\n### Architecture docs already present\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n\nReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n\n| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n\nThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n\nCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n\n- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n\n**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n\n| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification discipline\n\nEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n\n- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n\nIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sequencing rationale\n\nThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n\n1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n\nNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n\nThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n\nThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2619:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:301:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01JraPN454XQxqXddhC3Pid5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017pUgKMNHs9AuKm96kkQfas","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/plan.md","content":"# Build Plan — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n\n**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n**Context:** [`context.md`](context.md)\n**Harness contract:** [`tasks.json`](tasks.json)\n**Tasks directory:** [`tasks/`](tasks/)\n**Reviews directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/` (created by review tasks during execution)\n**Total tasks:** 89 (66 work tasks + 18 reviews + 5 contract/gate reviews) — see [`tasks.json`](tasks.json)\n**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n\n---\n\n## 1. Reading order\n\nIf you are an executor picking up this plan cold, read in this order:\n\n1. The spec — [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md). Don't summarize it; read it.\n2. The context file — [`context.md`](context.md). Carry-forward inventory, gap inventory, fork-or-extend evidence, verification discipline.\n3. This plan — for sequencing and review boundaries.\n4. The first unexecuted task file under [`tasks/`](tasks/).\n5. [`tasks.json`](tasks.json) only as the machine-readable contract for the harness, not as documentation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Phase-by-phase sequencing\n\nThe eleven spec phases drive the task ordering. Every phase ends in a review (`Rxx`). Contract-gate reviews sit at boundaries where multiple substrates interlock.\n\n| Phase | Spec § | Task IDs | Closing review | Why this order |\n|---|---y learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n\n### ViLoMem\n\nViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n\n- code topology\n- workflow episodes\n- failure patterns\n- semantic repo claims\n- architecture decisions\n- user/team preferences\n- docs and contract state\n\nEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n\n### SRMA-Style Reflection\n\nUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n\n- reflection quality\n- consolidation quality\n- retention quality\n- drift detection\n- retrieval alignment\n\nDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n\n- Local-first by default.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence.\n- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Ingestion Layer\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- watch workspace changes\n- parse code\n- parse docs\n- parse config and build files\n- ingest test results and diagnostics\n- capture assistant event traces\n- normalize all inputs into stable records\n\nRequired inputs:\n\n- source files\n- Markdown docs\n- repo instruction files\n- test files and test manifests\n- package/build metadata\n- compiler diagnostics\n- test runner output\n- MCP tool calls and responses\n- assistant edits and generated diffs\n- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n\nThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n\n### 2. Workspace Graph\n\nThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n\nPrimary node families:\n\n- `File`\n- `Directory`\n- `Symbol`\n- `Type`\n- `Module`\n- `Test`\n- `Document`\n- `Section`\n- `ConfigKey`\n- `Command`\n- `Route`\n- `Schema`\n- `Package`\n- `BuildTarget`\n- `RuntimeSurface`\n\nPrimary edge families:\n\n- `contains`\n- `imports`\n- `calls`\n- `implements`\n- `extends`\n- `type_ref`\n- `tested_by`\n- `documents`\n- `mentions`\n- `depends_on`\n- `configured_by`\n- `generated_by`\n- `co_changed_with`\n- `stale_against`\n\nThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n\n### 3. Event Log\n\nThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n\nRequired event types:\n\n- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n- `ToolCalled`\n- `ToolResult`\n- `ContextBundleReturned`\n- `MemoryRetrieved`\n- `MemoryExpanded`\n- `PlanCreated`\n- `FileRead`\n- `PatchApplied`\n- `TestRunStarted`\n- `TestRunCompleted`\n- `DiagnosticObserved`\n- `UserCorrection`\n- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n- `WorkflowFailed`\n- `MemoryCreated`\n- `MemoryUpdated`\n- `MemoryInvalidated`\n- `MemoryConsolidated`\n\nEvents should carry:\n\n- workspace id\n- branch\n- session id\n- task id\n- actor\n- timestamp\n- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n- payload hash\n- compact payload summary\n- optional full payload location\n\nThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n\n### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n\nConsolidation modes:\n\n- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n\nNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n\nLLM-driven consolidation:\n\nSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n\n- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n\n### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n\nVerification checks:\n\n- linked files still exist\n- linked symbols still exist\n- cited docs still exist\n- linked tests still exist\n- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n- time-bound memory has expired\n\nVerification outputs:\n\n- `verified`\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n\n### 9. MCP Surface\n\nThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n\nRequired workflow tools:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`\n- `prepare_change`\n- `plan_edit`\n- `trace_scenario`\n- `diagnose_failure`\n- `find_relevant_tests`\n- `impact_from_diff`\n- `get_docs_capsule`\n- `get_backlinks`\n- `expand_context`\n- `get_working_set_context`\n- `get_repo_playbook`\n- `search_symbols`\n- `search_memory`\n- `record_workflow_outcome`\n\nNew or redesigned memory tools:\n\n- `get_task_memory`\n- `save_memory`\n- `propose_memory_evolution`\n- `apply_memory_evolution`\n- `verify_memory`\n- `explain_memory`\n- `list_memory_conflicts`\n- `consolidate_session`\n- `get_memory_metrics`\n- `get_event_trace`\n\nTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n\nEvery tool response should support:\n\n- compact rendering\n- full structured JSON\n- context handles\n- stable expansion targets\n- budget controls\n- diagnostic explanations where useful\n\n### 10. Human Review Surface\n\nAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n\nRequired operator views:\n\n- memory inbox\n- proposed promotions\n- proposed contradictions\n- stale memory list\n- memory evidence view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- usefulness metrics\n- workspace graph health\n- indexing health\n- consolidation queue\n\nThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n\n## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n\n### Phase 11: Hardening\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- large-repo performance tests\n- concurrency tests\n- recovery tests\n- corrupted-event handling\n- partial-index handling\n- workspace-boundary tests\n- migration tests\n- MCP schema compatibility tests\n- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n\n## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n\nEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n\n- `overview`\n- ranked pivots\n- relevant context\n- memory highlights\n- event episodes where relevant\n- suggested next expansion\n- stable handles\n- risks or uncertainty\n- compact/full render choice\n- structured payload\n\nEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n\n- why the memory was included\n- evidence strength\n- verification status\n- freshness status\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- scope\n- expansion target\n\nEvery workflow tool should record:\n\n- inputs\n- resolved anchors\n- selected candidates\n- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n- response summary\n- downstream use events\n\n## Documentation Requirements\n\nRequired docs:\n\n- successor architecture overview\n- MCP contract reference\n- memory model reference\n- event log design\n- consolidation design\n- retrieval/ranking design\n- verification/freshness design\n- operator guide\n- migration guide from Lattice\n- benchmark/evaluation guide\n- extension review UI guide\n\nDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\nRequired test families:\n\n- storage migration tests\n- event append/replay tests\n- graph identity tests\n- parser tests\n- memory link tests\n- contradiction/supersession tests\n- stale verification tests\n- retrieval ranking golden tests\n- workflow golden tests\n- MCP schema tests\n- compact/full render tests\n- concurrency tests\n- large fixture performance tests\n- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n\nNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n\n## Measurable Success Criteria\n\nThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n\n- lower tool calls per coding task\n- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n- higher memory reuse precision\n- lower stale memory surfacing\n- better repeated-failure avoidance\n- better test recommendation accuracy\n- better user correction retention\n- faster diagnosis from failures\n- more explainable context selection\n\nInitial targets:\n\n- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n\n## Risks\n\n### Opaque Memory Mutation\n\nRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n\nControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n\n### Ranking Complexity\n\nRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n\nControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n\n### Event Log Growth\n\nRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n\nControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n\n### Stale Memory Leakage\n\nRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n\nControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n\n### Scope Leakage\n\nRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n\nControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n\n### Overfitting To Current Repo\n\nRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n\nControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n\nImplement in this order:\n\n1. Event model and append-only store.\n2. MCP workflow event capture.\n3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n4. Memory access/use history.\n5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n\nThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n\n## Final Target\n\nThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n\n- what the repo contains\n- why the repo is shaped that way\n- what was tried before\n- what failed before\n- what tests prove behavior\n- what memories are trustworthy\n- what memories are stale\n- what context matters now\n- what should be done next\n\nThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n","numLines":969,"startLine":1,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T40.log:7278:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2019:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4161:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T03.log:2725:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:111:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018zsq4mM4akuU6Hs8yDzpsz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"0fa88678-a6cb-4169-8c73-9d1c7278b0f0","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.168Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T40.log:7279:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2020:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4162:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T03.log:2809:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:293:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01UVmYnr3gHaH6rTbzMc6EN9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0147XV4VLMFkb6ECM3CEQHZg","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md","content":"# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n\n**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n\nThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the spec actually demands\n\nThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n\nThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n\n**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n\n- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n\n### Daemon (Rust)\n\n- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n\n### MCP / RPC\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n\n### Extension (TypeScript)\n\n- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n\n### Architecture docs already present\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n\nReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n\n| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n\nThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n\nCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n\n- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n\n**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n\n| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification discipline\n\nEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n\n- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n\nIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sequencing rationale\n\nThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n\n1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n\nNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n\nThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n\nThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":681,"cache_read_input_tokens":85471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":681},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2f8ef79d-b4b6-49da-8599-4f462a836578","request_id":"req_011Cb7LwMJ7arNp4ALKuNFMw"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T40.log:7280:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2021:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4163:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T03.log:2810:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:294:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0147XV4VLMFkb6ECM3CEQHZg","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"b1ef366b-0493-47fa-af95-c66339ba4c7f","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:24:01.804Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md","content":"# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n\n**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n\nThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the spec actually demands\n\nThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n\nThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n\n**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n\n- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n\n### Daemon (Rust)\n\n- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n\n### MCP / RPC\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n\n### Extension (TypeScript)\n\n- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n\n### Architecture docs already present\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n\nReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n\n| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n\nThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n\nCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n\n- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n\n**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n\n| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification discipline\n\nEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n\n- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n\nIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sequencing rationale\n\nThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n\n1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n\nNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n\nThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n\nThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T40.log:7282:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2023:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4189:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:12:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QR57zz5vCjWm6Nh812Nykt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n805\t\n806\t- `overview`\n807\t- ranked pivots\n808\t- relevant context\n809\t- memory highlights\n810\t- event episodes where relevant\n811\t- suggested next expansion\n812\t- stable handles\n813\t- risks or uncertainty\n814\t- compact/full render choice\n815\t- structured payload\n816\t\n817\tEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n818\t\n819\t- why the memory was included\n820\t- evidence strength\n821\t- verification status\n822\t- freshness status\n823\t- contradiction/supersession state\n824\t- scope\n825\t- expansion target\n826\t\n827\tEvery workflow tool should record:\n828\t\n829\t- inputs\n830\t- resolved anchors\n831\t- selected candidates\n832\t- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n833\t- response summary\n834\t- downstream use events\n835\t\n836\t## Documentation Requirements\n837\t\n838\tRequired docs:\n839\t\n840\t- successor architecture overview\n841\t- MCP contract reference\n842\t- memory model reference\n843\t- event log design\n844\t- consolidation design\n845\t- retrieval/ranking design\n846\t- verification/freshness design\n847\t- operator guide\n848\t- migration guide from Lattice\n849\t- benchmark/evaluation guide\n850\t- extension review UI guide\n851\t\n852\tDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n853\t\n854\t## Testing Requirements\n855\t\n856\tRequired test families:\n857\t\n858\t- storage migration tests\n859\t- event append/replay tests\n860\t- graph identity tests\n861\t- parser tests\n862\t- memory link tests\n863\t- contradiction/supersession tests\n864\t- stale verification tests\n865\t- retrieval ranking golden tests\n866\t- workflow golden tests\n867\t- MCP schema tests\n868\t- compact/full render tests\n869\t- concurrency tests\n870\t- large fixture performance tests\n871\t- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n872\t\n873\tNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n874\t\n875\t## Measurable Success Criteria\n876\t\n877\tThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n878\t\n879\t- lower tool calls per coding task\n880\t- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n881\t- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n882\t- higher memory reuse precision\n883\t- lower stale memory surfacing\n884\t- better repeated-failure avoidance\n885\t- better test recommendation accuracy\n886\t- better user correction retention\n887\t- faster diagnosis from failures\n888\t- more explainable context selection\n889\t\n890\tInitial targets:\n891\t\n892\t- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n893\t- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n894\t- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n895\t- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n896\t- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n897\t- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n898\t\n899\t## Risks\n900\t\n901\t### Opaque Memory Mutation\n902\t\n903\tRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n904\t\n905\tControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n906\t\n907\t### Ranking Complexity\n908\t\n909\tRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n910\t\n911\tControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n912\t\n913\t### Event Log Growth\n914\t\n915\tRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n916\t\n917\tControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n918\t\n919\t### Stale Memory Leakage\n920\t\n921\tRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n922\t\n923\tControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n924\t\n925\t### Scope Leakage\n926\t\n927\tRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n928\t\n929\tControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n930\t\n931\t### Overfitting To Current Repo\n932\t\n933\tRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n934\t\n935\tControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n936\t\n937\t## First Implementation Slice\n938\t\n939\tThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n940\t\n941\tImplement in this order:\n942\t\n943\t1. Event model and append-only store.\n944\t2. MCP workflow event capture.\n945\t3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n946\t4. Memory access/use history.\n947\t5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n948\t6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n949\t7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n950\t8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n951\t\n952\tThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n953\t\n954\t## Final Target\n955\t\n956\tThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n957\t\n958\t- what the repo contains\n959\t- why the repo is shaped that way\n960\t- what was tried before\n961\t- what failed before\n962\t- what tests prove behavior\n963\t- what memories are trustworthy\n964\t- what memories are stale\n965\t- what context matters now\n966\t- what should be done next\n967\t\n968\tThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n969\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"50e142df-b3bd-4b49-9ccf-0fed1541f6a9","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:17:40.069Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-05-16\n**Status:** Proposed\n**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n\nThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n\nThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n\n- code graph\n- document graph\n- event log\n- typed memory graph\n- workflow engine\n- consolidation engine\n- verification engine\n- explainable retrieval and ranking\n- assistant usefulness metrics\n\nThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n\n## Design Thesis\n\nThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n\n1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n\nThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n\n## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n\n### A-Mem\n\nA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n\n- atomic memory notes\n- generated context, tags, and keywords\n- embeddings for semantic access\n- explicit memory-to-memory links\n- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n\nAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n\n### AgeMem\n\nAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n\n- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n\nDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n\n### ViLoMem\n\nViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n\n- code topology\n- workflow episodes\n- failure patterns\n- semantic repo claims\n- architecture decisions\n- user/team preferences\n- docs and contract state\n\nEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n\n### SRMA-Style Reflection\n\nUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n\n- reflection quality\n- consolidation quality\n- retention quality\n- drift detection\n- retrieval alignment\n\nDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n\n- Local-first by default.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence.\n- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n\n## System Architecture\n\n### 1. Ingestion Layer\n\nResponsibilities:\n\n- watch workspace changes\n- parse code\n- parse docs\n- parse config and build files\n- ingest test results and diagnostics\n- capture assistant event traces\n- normalize all inputs into stable records\n\nRequired inputs:\n\n- source files\n- Markdown docs\n- repo instruction files\n- test files and test manifests\n- package/build metadata\n- compiler diagnostics\n- test runner output\n- MCP tool calls and responses\n- assistant edits and generated diffs\n- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n\nThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n\n### 2. Workspace Graph\n\nThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n\nPrimary node families:\n\n- `File`\n- `Directory`\n- `Symbol`\n- `Type`\n- `Module`\n- `Test`\n- `Document`\n- `Section`\n- `ConfigKey`\n- `Command`\n- `Route`\n- `Schema`\n- `Package`\n- `BuildTarget`\n- `RuntimeSurface`\n\nPrimary edge families:\n\n- `contains`\n- `imports`\n- `calls`\n- `implements`\n- `extends`\n- `type_ref`\n- `tested_by`\n- `documents`\n- `mentions`\n- `depends_on`\n- `configured_by`\n- `generated_by`\n- `co_changed_with`\n- `stale_against`\n\nThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n\n### 3. Event Log\n\nThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n\nRequired event types:\n\n- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n- `ToolCalled`\n- `ToolResult`\n- `ContextBundleReturned`\n- `MemoryRetrieved`\n- `MemoryExpanded`\n- `PlanCreated`\n- `FileRead`\n- `PatchApplied`\n- `TestRunStarted`\n- `TestRunCompleted`\n- `DiagnosticObserved`\n- `UserCorrection`\n- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n- `WorkflowFailed`\n- `MemoryCreated`\n- `MemoryUpdated`\n- `MemoryInvalidated`\n- `MemoryConsolidated`\n\nEvents should carry:\n\n- workspace id\n- branch\n- session id\n- task id\n- actor\n- timestamp\n- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n- payload hash\n- compact payload summary\n- optional full payload location\n\nThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n\n### 4. Memory Graph\n\nMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n\nPrimary memory classes:\n\n- `Observation`\n- `Decision`\n- `Constraint`\n- `Pattern`\n- `AntiPattern`\n- `WorkflowOutcome`\n- `FailurePattern`\n- `Procedure`\n- `Preference`\n- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n- `DocsContract`\n- `OpenQuestion`\n- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n\nEach memory record requires:\n\n- stable id\n- content\n- memory class\n- assertion type\n- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n- verification status\n- confidence and confidence reason\n- freshness policy\n- validity conditions\n- invalidation triggers\n- provenance events\n- evidence references\n- linked files\n- linked symbols\n- linked docs\n- linked tests\n- linked memories\n- contradiction links\n- supersession links\n- access history\n- usefulness scores\n- last verified state\n\nMemory links should be first-class records:\n\n- source memory\n- target memory or graph node\n- link type\n- strength\n- reason\n- evidence event\n- created by\n- created at\n- verification status\n\nRequired link types:\n\n- `supports`\n- `contradicts`\n- `supersedes`\n- `refines`\n- `generalizes`\n- `specializes`\n- `co_occurs_with`\n- `derived_from`\n- `applies_to`\n- `validated_by`\n- `invalidated_by`\n\n### 5. Working Memory\n\nWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n\nWorking memory contains:\n\n- task statement\n- interpreted intent\n- active files and symbols\n- active hypotheses\n- active failures\n- current plan\n- selected memories\n- excluded memories and reasons\n- budget decisions\n- unresolved questions\n- verification status\n\nRequired operations:\n\n- `retrieve`\n- `summarize`\n- `filter`\n- `pin`\n- `evict`\n- `expand`\n- `compress`\n- `checkpoint`\n\nWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n\n### 6. Consolidation Engine\n\nThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n\nConsolidation jobs:\n\n- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n- detect duplicate memories\n- detect contradictions\n- detect supersession candidates\n- demote unused or low-value memories\n- mark stale memories after graph changes\n- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n\nConsolidation modes:\n\n- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n\nNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n\nLLM-driven consolidation:\n\nSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n\n- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n\n### 7. Retrieval Engine\n\nRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n\nPipeline:\n\n1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n8. Score candidates.\n9. Deduplicate and compress.\n10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n\nCandidate sources:\n\n- exact path/symbol lookup\n- code graph traversal\n- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n- FTS\n- embeddings\n- event similarity\n- memory links\n- workflow similarity\n- recent active working memory\n\nRanking signals:\n\n- task-type compatibility\n- graph proximity to anchors\n- exact identifier match\n- semantic similarity\n- verification status\n- freshness\n- scope\n- evidence strength\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- past usefulness\n- recent successful reuse\n- user preference compatibility\n- token cost\n\nThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n\n### 8. Verification Engine\n\nThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n\nVerification checks:\n\n- linked files still exist\n- linked symbols still exist\n- cited docs still exist\n- linked tests still exist\n- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n- time-bound memory has expired\n\nVerification outputs:\n\n- `verified`\n- `unverified`\n- `in_review`\n- `stale`\n- `contradicted`\n- `superseded`\n- `expired`\n- `invalidated`\n\nVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n\n### 9. MCP Surface\n\nThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n\nRequired workflow tools:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`\n- `prepare_change`\n- `plan_edit`\n- `trace_scenario`\n- `diagnose_failure`\n- `find_relevant_tests`\n- `impact_from_diff`\n- `get_docs_capsule`\n- `get_backlinks`\n- `expand_context`\n- `get_working_set_context`\n- `get_repo_playbook`\n- `search_symbols`\n- `search_memory`\n- `record_workflow_outcome`\n\nNew or redesigned memory tools:\n\n- `get_task_memory`\n- `save_memory`\n- `propose_memory_evolution`\n- `apply_memory_evolution`\n- `verify_memory`\n- `explain_memory`\n- `list_memory_conflicts`\n- `consolidate_session`\n- `get_memory_metrics`\n- `get_event_trace`\n\nTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n\nEvery tool response should support:\n\n- compact rendering\n- full structured JSON\n- context handles\n- stable expansion targets\n- budget controls\n- diagnostic explanations where useful\n\n### 10. Human Review Surface\n\nAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n\nRequired operator views:\n\n- memory inbox\n- proposed promotions\n- proposed contradictions\n- stale memory list\n- memory evidence view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- usefulness metrics\n- workspace graph health\n- indexing health\n- consolidation queue\n\nThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n\n## Storage Design\n\nUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n\nCore tables:\n\n- `workspaces`\n- `files`\n- `symbols`\n- `documents`\n- `sections`\n- `tests`\n- `graph_edges`\n- `events`\n- `event_payloads`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_links`\n- `memory_evidence`\n- `memory_accesses`\n- `memory_scores`\n- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n- `context_handles`\n- `consolidation_jobs`\n- `verification_jobs`\n\nIndexes:\n\n- graph identity indexes\n- file path indexes\n- symbol name/path indexes\n- doc section indexes\n- event task/session indexes\n- memory scope/type/status indexes\n- memory link source/target indexes\n- freshness and verification indexes\n- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n\nThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n\nEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n\n## Fork Strategy\n\nThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n\nCarry forward:\n\n- Rust daemon structure\n- MCP handling experience\n- workspace boundary protections\n- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n- context handles\n- compact/full render modes\n- structured memory groundwork\n- test discipline around daemon behavior\n- VS Code extension integration pattern\n\nReplace or redesign:\n\n- memory retrieval as keyword search\n- memory as mostly isolated rows\n- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n\n## Implementation Plan\n\n### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n- architecture docs copied and revised\n- crate/module boundary plan\n- storage migration policy\n- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n\nFork decision gate:\n\nPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n\n### Phase 11: Hardening\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- large-repo performance tests\n- concurrency tests\n- recovery tests\n- corrupted-event handling\n- partial-index handling\n- workspace-boundary tests\n- migration tests\n- MCP schema compatibility tests\n- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n\n## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n\nEvery assistant-facing tool should return:\n\n- `overview`\n- ranked pivots\n- relevant context\n- memory highlights\n- event episodes where relevant\n- suggested next expansion\n- stable handles\n- risks or uncertainty\n- compact/full render choice\n- structured payload\n\nEvery memory-bearing response should identify:\n\n- why the memory was included\n- evidence strength\n- verification status\n- freshness status\n- contradiction/supersession state\n- scope\n- expansion target\n\nEvery workflow tool should record:\n\n- inputs\n- resolved anchors\n- selected candidates\n- excluded high-scoring candidates where useful\n- response summary\n- downstream use events\n\n## Documentation Requirements\n\nRequired docs:\n\n- successor architecture overview\n- MCP contract reference\n- memory model reference\n- event log design\n- consolidation design\n- retrieval/ranking design\n- verification/freshness design\n- operator guide\n- migration guide from Lattice\n- benchmark/evaluation guide\n- extension review UI guide\n\nDocs must be updated in the same change as public behavior changes.\n\n## Testing Requirements\n\nRequired test families:\n\n- storage migration tests\n- event append/replay tests\n- graph identity tests\n- parser tests\n- memory link tests\n- contradiction/supersession tests\n- stale verification tests\n- retrieval ranking golden tests\n- workflow golden tests\n- MCP schema tests\n- compact/full render tests\n- concurrency tests\n- large fixture performance tests\n- extension compile/UI smoke tests\n\nNo phase is complete until its failure paths are tested.\n\n## Measurable Success Criteria\n\nThe fork should beat current Lattice on:\n\n- lower tool calls per coding task\n- lower irrelevant context inclusion\n- higher relevant file/symbol recall\n- higher memory reuse precision\n- lower stale memory surfacing\n- better repeated-failure avoidance\n- better test recommendation accuracy\n- better user correction retention\n- faster diagnosis from failures\n- more explainable context selection\n\nInitial targets:\n\n- 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n- 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n- 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n- zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n- zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n- 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n\n## Risks\n\n### Opaque Memory Mutation\n\nRisk: automatic consolidation corrupts durable repo knowledge.\n\nControl: event-backed proposals, reversible updates, provenance, review queue for high-scope changes, and replay.\n\n### Ranking Complexity\n\nRisk: ranking becomes hard to reason about.\n\nControl: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\n\n### Event Log Growth\n\nRisk: event storage becomes too large or slow.\n\nControl: append compact summaries on hot paths, spill large payloads, index by task/session/workspace, and support compaction snapshots.\n\n### Stale Memory Leakage\n\nRisk: agents trust outdated memory.\n\nControl: freshness indexes, graph-change-triggered verification, stale labels in all memory surfaces, and tests that stale memory cannot rank as trusted.\n\n### Scope Leakage\n\nRisk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces.\n\nControl: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\n\n### Overfitting To Current Repo\n\nRisk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos.\n\nControl: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nThe first real slice should be event log plus memory graph, not UI.\n\nImplement in this order:\n\n1. Event model and append-only store.\n2. MCP workflow event capture.\n3. Memory graph schema and migration/import from existing rows.\n4. Memory access/use history.\n5. Retrieval V1 with graph-aware memory ranking.\n6. Compact memory inclusion reasons in existing workflow tools.\n7. Session consolidation into episode memories.\n8. Verification and stale surfacing tests.\n\nThis slice creates the substrate that all later intelligence depends on. Building UI or learned policy before this substrate would create debt instead of capability.\n\n## Final Target\n\nThe final system should make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately:\n\n- what the repo contains\n- why the repo is shaped that way\n- what was tried before\n- what failed before\n- what tests prove behavior\n- what memories are trustworthy\n- what memories are stale\n- what context matters now\n- what should be done next\n\nThat is the measurable leap over a code graph plus ad hoc memory store.\n","numLines":969,"startLine":1,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T40.log:7283:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2024:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4191:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:111:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018zsq4mM4akuU6Hs8yDzpsz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cognitive Workspace Fork Plan\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-05-16\n4\t**Status:** Proposed\n5\t**Audience:** Lattice maintainers considering a fork or major successor architecture\n6\t**Source Context:** Lattice current code graph, workflow tools, structured memory, A-Mem, AgeMem, ViLoMem, and SRMA-style memory/reflection concepts\n7\t\n8\t## Goal\n9\t\n10\tBuild a mature assistant-facing workspace cognition system from Lattice's strongest primitives, without constraining the design to an incremental Lattice feature extension.\n11\t\n12\tThe target system should let coding agents understand a codebase, act on it, remember what happened, consolidate useful experience, verify whether memories still hold, and retrieve the right context with inspectable reasons.\n13\t\n14\tThe core product should be a local MCP server and daemon that exposes a unified engineering intelligence layer:\n15\t\n16\t- code graph\n17\t- document graph\n18\t- event log\n19\t- typed memory graph\n20\t- workflow engine\n21\t- consolidation engine\n22\t- verification engine\n23\t- explainable retrieval and ranking\n24\t- assistant usefulness metrics\n25\t\n26\tThe bar is not \"RAG over a repo.\" The bar is a trustworthy, auditable, long-running engineering memory system for agents.\n27\t\n28\t## Design Thesis\n29\t\n30\tThe correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth:\n31\t\n32\t1. **Workspace graph:** files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries.\n33\t2. **Event log:** every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome.\n34\t3. **Memory graph:** durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\n35\t\n36\tThese substrates should share identity, storage, and ranking primitives. A memory is not just text. It is a claim with evidence and validity conditions.\n37\t\n38\t## Research Concepts To Carry Forward\n39\t\n40\t### A-Mem\n41\t\n42\tA-Mem contributes the most directly useful memory model:\n43\t\n44\t- atomic memory notes\n45\t- generated context, tags, and keywords\n46\t- embeddings for semantic access\n47\t- explicit memory-to-memory links\n48\t- memory evolution as new notes arrive\n49\t\n50\tAdopt the structure and link evolution. Do not adopt silent uncontrolled mutation. In this system, memory evolution should produce auditable proposals or transactional updates with provenance.\n51\t\n52\t### AgeMem\n53\t\n54\tAgeMem is most useful as an operation and evaluation model:\n55\t\n56\t- separate short-term and long-term memory actions\n57\t- `ADD`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` for durable memory\n58\t- `RETRIEVE`, `SUMMARY`, and `FILTER` for working memory\n59\t- delayed usefulness signals for memory policy quality\n60\t\n61\tDo not start with reinforcement learning. First build deterministic event capture, retrieval telemetry, and offline usefulness scoring. Policy learning can only be credible after the substrate records enough real outcomes.\n62\t\n63\t### ViLoMem\n64\t\n65\tViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams:\n66\t\n67\t- code topology\n68\t- workflow episodes\n69\t- failure patterns\n70\t- semantic repo claims\n71\t- architecture decisions\n72\t- user/team preferences\n73\t- docs and contract state\n74\t\n75\tEach stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\n76\t\n77\t### SRMA-Style Reflection\n78\t\n79\tUse SRMA-like concepts as quality metrics:\n80\t\n81\t- reflection quality\n82\t- consolidation quality\n83\t- retention quality\n84\t- drift detection\n85\t- retrieval alignment\n86\t\n87\tDo not build opaque autonomous self-rewriting memory. Reflection should be inspectable, testable, and reversible.\n88\t\n89\t## Non-Negotiable Product Properties\n90\t\n91\t- Local-first by default.\n92\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n93\t- Every durable memory has evidence.\n94\t- Every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n95\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced as such, not hidden behind recency.\n96\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default with deliberate expansion handles.\n97\t- Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\n98\t- Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\n99\t- No silent broad workspace reads that bypass ignore rules or workspace boundaries.\n100\t- No unbounded graph traversal, payload growth, or event-log scans on hot paths.\n101\t\n102\t## System Architecture\n103\t\n104\t### 1. Ingestion Layer\n105\t\n106\tResponsibilities:\n107\t\n108\t- watch workspace changes\n109\t- parse code\n110\t- parse docs\n111\t- parse config and build files\n112\t- ingest test results and diagnostics\n113\t- capture assistant event traces\n114\t- normalize all inputs into stable records\n115\t\n116\tRequired inputs:\n117\t\n118\t- source files\n119\t- Markdown docs\n120\t- repo instruction files\n121\t- test files and test manifests\n122\t- package/build metadata\n123\t- compiler diagnostics\n124\t- test runner output\n125\t- MCP tool calls and responses\n126\t- assistant edits and generated diffs\n127\t- user corrections and accepted/rejected outcomes\n128\t\n129\tThe ingestion layer should never directly decide what context is important. It produces normalized facts and events.\n130\t\n131\t### 2. Workspace Graph\n132\t\n133\tThe workspace graph stores code, docs, tests, and operational surfaces.\n134\t\n135\tPrimary node families:\n136\t\n137\t- `File`\n138\t- `Directory`\n139\t- `Symbol`\n140\t- `Type`\n141\t- `Module`\n142\t- `Test`\n143\t- `Document`\n144\t- `Section`\n145\t- `ConfigKey`\n146\t- `Command`\n147\t- `Route`\n148\t- `Schema`\n149\t- `Package`\n150\t- `BuildTarget`\n151\t- `RuntimeSurface`\n152\t\n153\tPrimary edge families:\n154\t\n155\t- `contains`\n156\t- `imports`\n157\t- `calls`\n158\t- `implements`\n159\t- `extends`\n160\t- `type_ref`\n161\t- `tested_by`\n162\t- `documents`\n163\t- `mentions`\n164\t- `depends_on`\n165\t- `configured_by`\n166\t- `generated_by`\n167\t- `co_changed_with`\n168\t- `stale_against`\n169\t\n170\tThe graph must support exact identity lookup, bounded neighborhood traversal, impact queries, doc/code backlinks, and freshness queries.\n171\t\n172\t### 3. Event Log\n173\t\n174\tThe event log is append-only and replayable. It is the substrate that makes memory consolidation and usefulness measurement possible.\n175\t\n176\tRequired event types:\n177\t\n178\t- `AssistantTaskStarted`\n179\t- `ToolCalled`\n180\t- `ToolResult`\n181\t- `ContextBundleReturned`\n182\t- `MemoryRetrieved`\n183\t- `MemoryExpanded`\n184\t- `PlanCreated`\n185\t- `FileRead`\n186\t- `PatchApplied`\n187\t- `TestRunStarted`\n188\t- `TestRunCompleted`\n189\t- `DiagnosticObserved`\n190\t- `UserCorrection`\n191\t- `UserPreferenceObserved`\n192\t- `WorkflowSucceeded`\n193\t- `WorkflowFailed`\n194\t- `MemoryCreated`\n195\t- `MemoryUpdated`\n196\t- `MemoryInvalidated`\n197\t- `MemoryConsolidated`\n198\t\n199\tEvents should carry:\n200\t\n201\t- workspace id\n202\t- branch\n203\t- session id\n204\t- task id\n205\t- actor\n206\t- timestamp\n207\t- stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories\n208\t- payload hash\n209\t- compact payload summary\n210\t- optional full payload location\n211\t\n212\tThe full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\n213\t\n214\t### 4. Memory Graph\n215\t\n216\tMemory should be stored as typed claims and episodes, not loose notes.\n217\t\n218\tPrimary memory classes:\n219\t\n220\t- `Observation`\n221\t- `Decision`\n222\t- `Constraint`\n223\t- `Pattern`\n224\t- `AntiPattern`\n225\t- `WorkflowOutcome`\n226\t- `FailurePattern`\n227\t- `Procedure`\n228\t- `Preference`\n229\t- `ArchitectureInvariant`\n230\t- `DocsContract`\n231\t- `OpenQuestion`\n232\t- `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated. Use CounterMemory when the counter-claim needs its own provenance and lifecycle, not just an edge annotation.\n233\t\n234\tEach memory record requires:\n235\t\n236\t- stable id\n237\t- content\n238\t- memory class\n239\t- assertion type\n240\t- scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\n241\t- verification status\n242\t- confidence and confidence reason\n243\t- freshness policy\n244\t- validity conditions\n245\t- invalidation triggers\n246\t- provenance events\n247\t- evidence references\n248\t- linked files\n249\t- linked symbols\n250\t- linked docs\n251\t- linked tests\n252\t- linked memories\n253\t- contradiction links\n254\t- supersession links\n255\t- access history\n256\t- usefulness scores\n257\t- last verified state\n258\t\n259\tMemory links should be first-class records:\n260\t\n261\t- source memory\n262\t- target memory or graph node\n263\t- link type\n264\t- strength\n265\t- reason\n266\t- evidence event\n267\t- created by\n268\t- created at\n269\t- verification status\n270\t\n271\tRequired link types:\n272\t\n273\t- `supports`\n274\t- `contradicts`\n275\t- `supersedes`\n276\t- `refines`\n277\t- `generalizes`\n278\t- `specializes`\n279\t- `co_occurs_with`\n280\t- `derived_from`\n281\t- `applies_to`\n282\t- `validated_by`\n283\t- `invalidated_by`\n284\t\n285\t### 5. Working Memory\n286\t\n287\tWorking memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation.\n288\t\n289\tWorking memory contains:\n290\t\n291\t- task statement\n292\t- interpreted intent\n293\t- active files and symbols\n294\t- active hypotheses\n295\t- active failures\n296\t- current plan\n297\t- selected memories\n298\t- excluded memories and reasons\n299\t- budget decisions\n300\t- unresolved questions\n301\t- verification status\n302\t\n303\tRequired operations:\n304\t\n305\t- `retrieve`\n306\t- `summarize`\n307\t- `filter`\n308\t- `pin`\n309\t- `evict`\n310\t- `expand`\n311\t- `compress`\n312\t- `checkpoint`\n313\t\n314\tWorking memory is not durable by itself. Durable value is extracted through event-backed consolidation.\n315\t\n316\t### 6. Consolidation Engine\n317\t\n318\tThe consolidation engine turns event traces into better memory.\n319\t\n320\tConsolidation jobs:\n321\t\n322\t- create episode summaries from completed tasks\n323\t- promote repeated successful workflow traces into procedures\n324\t- promote recurring failures into failure patterns\n325\t- promote verified implementation facts into semantic repo memory\n326\t- detect duplicate memories\n327\t- detect contradictions\n328\t- detect supersession candidates\n329\t- demote unused or low-value memories\n330\t- mark stale memories after graph changes\n331\t- refresh memories whose evidence still matches current code\n332\t- propose docs updates when memory and docs diverge\n333\t\n334\tConsolidation modes:\n335\t\n336\t- synchronous post-task consolidation for small task traces\n337\t- background scheduled consolidation for larger histories\n338\t- manual review mode for high-impact repo or organization memories\n339\t- replay mode for rebuilding memory state from the event log\n340\t\n341\tNo consolidation job may silently rewrite high-scope memory without preserving provenance and prior state.\n342\t\n343\tLLM-driven consolidation:\n344\t\n345\tSeveral consolidation jobs (episode summary generation, procedure extraction, contradiction detection, failure-pattern extraction) cannot be implemented deterministically and require LLM inference. These jobs must follow additional constraints:\n346\t\n347\t- each LLM-driven job produces a proposal record, not a direct write; proposals require explicit apply or reject before altering durable memory\n348\t- failed or malformed LLM responses must leave the prior memory state unchanged and emit a consolidation failure event\n349\t- LLM-driven jobs run only in background or manual review modes, never on the synchronous post-task hot path\n350\t- each job records its model, prompt hash, and response hash as part of the proposal provenance so outputs are auditable and reproducible\n351\t- consolidation queue depth must be bounded; when the queue is full, new jobs are dropped with a log warning rather than stalling the daemon\n352\t- cost and latency budgets for LLM consolidation should be documented per job type before Phase 6 begins; jobs exceeding budget must fall back to deterministic approximations or skip with a stale flag\n353\t\n354\t### 7. Retrieval Engine\n355\t\n356\tRetrieval must be task-aware and explainable.\n357\t\n358\tPipeline:\n359\t\n360\t1. Parse user task and classify intent.\n361\t2. Extract literal anchors: paths, symbols, errors, commands, APIs, config keys.\n362\t3. Resolve anchors into graph identities.\n363\t4. Retrieve graph candidates.\n364\t5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams.\n365\t6. Retrieve relevant event episodes.\n366\t7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods.\n367\t8. Score candidates.\n368\t9. Deduplicate and compress.\n369\t10. Return a compact bundle with inclusion reasons and expansion handles.\n370\t\n371\tCandidate sources:\n372\t\n373\t- exact path/symbol lookup\n374\t- code graph traversal\n375\t- doc backlinks/outgoing links\n376\t- FTS\n377\t- embeddings\n378\t- event similarity\n379\t- memory links\n380\t- workflow similarity\n381\t- recent active working memory\n382\t\n383\tRanking signals:\n384\t\n385\t- task-type compatibility\n386\t- graph proximity to anchors\n387\t- exact identifier match\n388\t- semantic similarity\n389\t- verification status\n390\t- freshness\n391\t- scope\n392\t- evidence strength\n393\t- contradiction/supersession state\n394\t- past usefulness\n395\t- recent successful reuse\n396\t- user preference compatibility\n397\t- token cost\n398\t\n399\tThe ranker should expose scores and reasons in diagnostic mode. Compact mode should include only enough explanation for an assistant to trust the result.\n400\t\n401\t### 8. Verification Engine\n402\t\n403\tThe verification engine keeps memory honest.\n404\t\n405\tVerification checks:\n406\t\n407\t- linked files still exist\n408\t- linked symbols still exist\n409\t- cited docs still exist\n410\t- linked tests still exist\n411\t- evidence text still matches when exact spans were captured\n412\t- implementation still matches memory claim where deterministic checks are possible\n413\t- contradicted/superseded states remain coherent\n414\t- branch-scoped memory is not leaking into unrelated branches\n415\t- time-bound memory has expired\n416\t\n417\tVerification outputs:\n418\t\n419\t- `verified`\n420\t- `unverified`\n421\t- `in_review`\n422\t- `stale`\n423\t- `contradicted`\n424\t- `superseded`\n425\t- `expired`\n426\t- `invalidated`\n427\t\n428\tVerification must be incremental and tied to workspace changes. Large repos cannot tolerate full rescans for every memory.\n429\t\n430\t### 9. MCP Surface\n431\t\n432\tThe MCP contract should expose high-level assistant workflows rather than forcing clients to compose low-level graph calls.\n433\t\n434\tRequired workflow tools:\n435\t\n436\t- `get_context_capsule`\n437\t- `prepare_change`\n438\t- `plan_edit`\n439\t- `trace_scenario`\n440\t- `diagnose_failure`\n441\t- `find_relevant_tests`\n442\t- `impact_from_diff`\n443\t- `get_docs_capsule`\n444\t- `get_backlinks`\n445\t- `expand_context`\n446\t- `get_working_set_context`\n447\t- `get_repo_playbook`\n448\t- `search_symbols`\n449\t- `search_memory`\n450\t- `record_workflow_outcome`\n451\t\n452\tNew or redesigned memory tools:\n453\t\n454\t- `get_task_memory`\n455\t- `save_memory`\n456\t- `propose_memory_evolution`\n457\t- `apply_memory_evolution`\n458\t- `verify_memory`\n459\t- `explain_memory`\n460\t- `list_memory_conflicts`\n461\t- `consolidate_session`\n462\t- `get_memory_metrics`\n463\t- `get_event_trace`\n464\t\n465\tTool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\n466\t\n467\tEvery tool response should support:\n468\t\n469\t- compact rendering\n470\t- full structured JSON\n471\t- context handles\n472\t- stable expansion targets\n473\t- budget controls\n474\t- diagnostic explanations where useful\n475\t\n476\t### 10. Human Review Surface\n477\t\n478\tAgents are the primary client, but durable memory needs human inspectability.\n479\t\n480\tRequired operator views:\n481\t\n482\t- memory inbox\n483\t- proposed promotions\n484\t- proposed contradictions\n485\t- stale memory list\n486\t- memory evidence view\n487\t- event trace view\n488\t- retrieval explanation view\n489\t- usefulness metrics\n490\t- workspace graph health\n491\t- indexing health\n492\t- consolidation queue\n493\t\n494\tThis can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\n495\t\n496\t## Storage Design\n497\t\n498\tUse SQLite as the durable local store unless proven insufficient. Split logical tables cleanly.\n499\t\n500\tCore tables:\n501\t\n502\t- `workspaces`\n503\t- `files`\n504\t- `symbols`\n505\t- `documents`\n506\t- `sections`\n507\t- `tests`\n508\t- `graph_edges`\n509\t- `events`\n510\t- `event_payloads`\n511\t- `memories`\n512\t- `memory_links`\n513\t- `memory_evidence`\n514\t- `memory_accesses`\n515\t- `memory_scores`\n516\t- `working_memory_checkpoints`\n517\t- `context_handles`\n518\t- `consolidation_jobs`\n519\t- `verification_jobs`\n520\t\n521\tIndexes:\n522\t\n523\t- graph identity indexes\n524\t- file path indexes\n525\t- symbol name/path indexes\n526\t- doc section indexes\n527\t- event task/session indexes\n528\t- memory scope/type/status indexes\n529\t- memory link source/target indexes\n530\t- freshness and verification indexes\n531\t- FTS over code summary, docs, memory, and event summaries\n532\t- vector indexes over symbols, doc sections, memories, and event episodes\n533\t\n534\tThe event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\n535\t\n536\tEvent log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\n537\t\n538\t## Fork Strategy\n539\t\n540\tThis should be a fork only if the implementation needs to break major internal assumptions. Preserve useful Lattice assets.\n541\t\n542\tCarry forward:\n543\t\n544\t- Rust daemon structure\n545\t- MCP handling experience\n546\t- workspace boundary protections\n547\t- existing workflow tool vocabulary\n548\t- graph parsing and indexing foundation\n549\t- context handles\n550\t- compact/full render modes\n551\t- structured memory groundwork\n552\t- test discipline around daemon behavior\n553\t- VS Code extension integration pattern\n554\t\n555\tReplace or redesign:\n556\t\n557\t- memory retrieval as keyword search\n558\t- memory as mostly isolated rows\n559\t- workflow metrics as secondary side data\n560\t- event capture as implicit logs instead of a first-class substrate\n561\t- ranking paths that cannot explain why context was included\n562\t- any storage path that cannot be replayed or verified\n563\t\n564\t## Implementation Plan\n565\t\n566\t### Phase 0: Fork Foundation\n567\t\n568\tDeliverables:\n569\t\n570\t- new repo or long-lived branch with clear successor name\n571\t- architecture docs copied and revised\n572\t- crate/module boundary plan\n573\t- storage migration policy\n574\t- compatibility policy for existing MCP tools\n575\t- baseline benchmark suite from current Lattice workflows\n576\t\n577\tFork decision gate:\n578\t\n579\tPhase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true:\n580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t\n780\tDefinition of done:\n781\t\n782\t- operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows\n783\t\n784\t### Phase 11: Hardening\n785\t\n786\tDeliverables:\n787\t\n788\t- large-repo performance tests\n789\t- concurrency tests\n790\t- recovery tests\n791\t- corrupted-event handling\n792\t- partial-index handling\n793\t- workspace-boundary tests\n794\t- migration tests\n795\t- MCP schema compatibility tests\n796\t- documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior\n797\t\n798\tDefinition of done:\n799\t\n800\t- the system is production-grade for repeated agent use, not only successful on a toy repo\n801\t\n802\t## MCP Tool Contract Principles\n803\t\n804\tEvery assistant-facing toopisodeMemory` rows for that task, then apply the proposal and assert the memory appears AND a `MemoryConsolidated` event is in the event log.\n4. Write `test_deterministic_supersession_proposal_apply_then_reject_round_trip` (covers T41): seed two overlapping memories, run `SupersessionCandidates::scan`, assert one `Supersede` proposal is pending, apply it, assert the older memory's status is `superseded` AND a `memory_links` edge points to the newer, then call `ReplayDriver::reverse(proposal_id)` and assert the status flips back and the link is gone.\n5. Write `test_llm_episode_job_well_formed_response_emits_proposal_with_provenance` (covers T42 + T43): script the fake driver to return a well-formed JSON episode summary, run `EpisodeSummaryJob::run` in `Background` mode, assert a proposal exists with `provenance.is_some()`, `provenance.model == \"fake-llm\"`, and matching `prompt_sha256` / `response_sha256`.\n6. Write `test_llm_malformed_response_leaves_memory_unchanged_and_emits_failure_event` (covers T42 malformed-handling): script the driver to return invalid JSON, assert zero proposals enqueued, zero memory mutations, and exactly one `ConsolidationFailed` event with `error_kind = MalformedResponse`.\n7. Write `test_llm_synchronous_mode_is_forbidden_and_driver_never_called` (covers T42 mode guard): attempt `EpisodeSummaryJob::run(ctx { mode: SynchronousPostTask }, …)`, assert it returns `Err(ForbiddenOnHotPath)`, assert the fake driver's call count is zero.\n8. Write `test_bounded_queue_overflow_drops_jobs_with_warning_event` (covers T39 + T43): set `max_depth = 2`, enqueue 5 jobs of the same LLM kind, assert exactly 3 are dropped, exactly 3 `ConsolidationFailed` events with `error_kind = QueueFull` are emitted, and the `tracing::warn!` log surfaced (capture via a tracing-test subscriber).\n9. Write `test_high_scope_proposal_routes_to_manual_review_queue` (covers T44): seed a `repo`-scope target memory, run a job that produces a proposal for it, assert the proposal is `pending` and visible via `ReviewQueue::list_pending(scope=Repo)`, assert it did NOT auto-apply.\n10. Write `test_replay_from_genesis_reconstructs_memory_state` (covers T45): apply 10 mixed proposals through the runtime, snapshot the memory store hash, reset the memory tables, run `ReplayDriver::replay(FromGenesis)` with the provenance cache, assert the post-replay memory hash equals the snapshot hash AND the live LLM driver was never called.\n11. Wire the suite via `mod integration_tests;` in `consolidation/mod.rs` under `#[cfg(test)]` (or a `tests/` sibling per crate convention — match `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/tests.rs` style).\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/integration_tests.rs` — `ConsolidationHarness` plus the named behavior-per-test functions above covering synchronous, deterministic, LLM well-formed, LLM malformed, mode guard, queue overflow, manual-review routing, and replay reconstruction.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/integration_tests.rs` — confirms the integration test file exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib consolidation::integration_tests` — every Phase 6 integration test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split harness into `integration_test_support.rs` if approaching the ceiling).\n- Test length ≤30 lines per `#[test]` function; setup factored into the harness.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 for harness helpers.\n- Single source of truth: tests exercise the real T39–T45 modules via their public APIs; no shadow re-implementation of proposal storage or event emission.\n- Naming: tests follow `test_<behavior_description>` per `coding.md` §Tests as documentation (e.g. `test_llm_malformed_response_leaves_memory_unchanged_and_emits_failure_event`).\n- Error handling: tests assert on typed outcomes (`EnqueueOutcome::Dropped`, `LlmJobError::ForbiddenOnHotPath`) rather than message strings.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Observability: tests capture `tracing::warn!` output via a test subscriber so warn-on-drop is verifiable, not just assumed.\n- Doc citation: file-top doc comment cites the four spec invariants verbatim (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] `integration_tests.rs` exists with every test from steps 3–10 implemented.\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied (in particular, no individual test exceeds 30 lines).\n- [ ] No deferred work: all four Phase 6 invariants are covered end-to-end in this task; no \"leave for later\" placeholders.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15541,"cache_read_input_tokens":56911,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15541,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015qAoQeXM7HFtFDzBbPkUNq","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"abe467b8-23c3-46c2-8c75-07c0acef06a4","request_id":"req_011Cb7N1yvFZe4LypqRsfPgL","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 6+7+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T40.log:7285:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2028:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4201:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2613:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:293:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01UVmYnr3gHaH6rTbzMc6EN9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0147XV4VLMFkb6ECM3CEQHZg","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md","content":"# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n\n**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n\nThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the spec actually demands\n\nThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n\nThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n\n**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n\n- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n\n### Daemon (Rust)\n\n- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n\n### MCP / RPC\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n\n### Extension (TypeScript)\n\n- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n\n### Architecture docs already present\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n\nReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n\n| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n\nThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n\nCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n\n- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n\n**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n\n| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification discipline\n\nEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n\n- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n\nIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sequencing rationale\n\nThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n\n1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n\nNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n\nThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n\nThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":681,"cache_read_input_tokens":85471,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":681},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2f8ef79d-b4b6-49da-8599-4f462a836578","request_id":"req_011Cb7LwMJ7arNp4ALKuNFMw"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T40.log:7286:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T37.log:2029:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T13.log:4202:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2614:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:294:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0147XV4VLMFkb6ECM3CEQHZg","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"b1ef366b-0493-47fa-af95-c66339ba4c7f","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:24:01.804Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md","content":"# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n\n**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n\nThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the spec actually demands\n\nThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n\nThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n\n**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n\n- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n\n### Daemon (Rust)\n\n- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n\n### MCP / RPC\n\n- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n\n### Extension (TypeScript)\n\n- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n\n### Architecture docs already present\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n\nReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n\n| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n\nThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n\nCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n\n- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n\n**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n\n| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Verification discipline\n\nEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n\n- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n\nIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sequencing rationale\n\nThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n\n1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n\nNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n\nThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n\nThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
